  "rule": "Lowest MAE of the five-seed ensemble on phase 2, then two bars before a cheaper configuration may displace it.\nThe first bar is statistical. Every pair of configurations is compared by a paired bootstrap over the 260 compounds, resampled on one shared set of draws so each difference is paired, and the family of pairs is corrected by Benjamini-Hochberg at a false discovery rate of 0.05. The leader's row of that family is the tied set. The family is every pair rather than only the comparisons against the leader, because a figure of nineteen bars invites comparing any two of them and because under a step-up procedure the larger family is the more powerful one here. Each verdict is recorded with its p-value and the threshold it was judged against.\nThat bar alone is too weak to act on, since at this sample size it fails to separate differences ten times the run-to-run noise, so a second bar applies: the gap to the leader must be no larger than the leader's own standard deviation across seeds, which is to say the saving must cost less than changing the training seed does. The cheapest configuration clearing both wins, cheap meaning fewer blocks and then fewer columns. The tied set, the affordable subset, the budget and the tie-break are all recorded.\nTwo limits this rule does not remove. Benjamini-Hochberg holds under independence or positive regression dependence, and comparisons on a shared compound set are correlated rather than independent; that is the case it is generally taken to cover, and it is not proven here. And the reference is the empirical leader, chosen by looking at the same data the comparisons use, so the tied sets are if anything narrower than they should be. No correction in this family addresses that.\n",
